What is broken today:

CI failures require manual investigation across many tools. Engineers jump between GitHub Actions logs, Jira, Confluence, repositories, and chat to understand one failure.
Useful knowledge exists but is hard to find. Runbooks, architecture notes, onboarding guides, and historical incident resolutions are scattered and disconnected.
Failure responses are often generic. Teams may simply rerun jobs or search broadly instead of getting a service-specific, evidence-backed next step.
Context is lost during handoffs. Engineers repeatedly explain the same incident, service ownership, and remediation history to leads or teammates.
New developers take longer to become productive. They must manually discover the right tools, entitlements, runbooks, and service contacts.
Engineering leads lack a focused view of delivery risk. Jira priorities, failed pipelines, pull requests, service ownership, and risk signals are spread across separate views.
AI answers may be difficult to trust. Ordinary chat can produce plausible advice without showing its evidence, source, or whether the answer came from a live model or fallback.
Automation can be risky without controls. Retrying jobs or proposing code changes without sandbox validation, risk checks, auditability, and human approval can make incidents worse.

Key Capabilities
CI Failure Investigation

Analyzes failed GitHub Actions jobs.
Connects logs with service context, runbooks, Jira incidents, and Confluence knowledge.
Recommends a specific, safe next step.
Grounded Engineering Copilot

Answers architecture, onboarding, ownership, tooling, and incident questions.
Uses RAG with PostgreSQL and pgvector.
Provides source references and shows whether the answer came from Claude or fallback evidence.
Supervised Self-Healing

Reproduces deterministic failures in an isolated sandbox.
Applies and tests a controlled code fix.
Uses risk and confidence gates before creating a draft pull request.
Requires human approval and never merges automatically.
Delivery Risk Management

Combines Jira priorities, pull requests, failed pipelines, ownership, and dependencies.
Gives engineering leads a focused view of work requiring attention.
Provides deterministic pull-request risk analysis.
Governed Automation

Supports Observe, Supervised, and Constrained modes.
Enforces repository allowlists, retry limits, risk thresholds, and human approval.
Displays live health and provenance for GitHub, Jira, Confluence, Claude, PostgreSQL, and RAG.

Technology Used
AI model: Claude through Azure AI Foundry for failure analysis, Copilot responses, and remediation reasoning.
Backend platform: Python, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, and Uvicorn.
Frontend: HTML, CSS, and JavaScript served by FastAPI.
Database: Azure PostgreSQL with the pgvector extension.
APIs: GitHub Actions/PR APIs, Jira Cloud REST API, Confluence API, and Azure AI Foundry Anthropic API.
Data sources: GitHub CI logs and pull requests, Jira issues, Confluence pages, repository documentation, service catalogs, runbooks, architecture documents, and ownership records.
Retrieval: RAG with vector similarity search, keyword search, and hybrid ranking.
Automation tools: Git, isolated temporary checkouts, pytest sandbox validation, fine-grained GitHub tokens, and draft pull requests.
Deployment: Windows Azure VM with a Python virtual environment and localhost Uvicorn service.

Reusability
Teams: SRE, DevOps, platform, security, data, and application teams.
CI platforms: GitHub Actions, Azure DevOps, Jenkins, GitLab CI, and CircleCI.
